<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Erfurt</h1><p class="st-meta">1806 CE – 1814 CE · capital Erfurt</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Thuringian city-state that Napoleon kept as his personal domain, scene of his 1808 congress with Tsar Alexander I.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a>, long ruled by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-mainz">Electorate of Mainz</a>, passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1802. After defeating Prussia at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> in October 1806, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> did not give Erfurt to any of his allies but kept it as a domain reserved to himself, the Principality of Erfurt, administered by French governors.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a> was governed directly by French officials and garrisoned by French troops, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petersberg-citadel">Petersberg</a> citadel as its stronghold. From September to October 1808 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-erfurt">Congress of Erfurt</a> there, meeting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Tsar Alexander I</a> to renew their alliance and receiving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-wolfgang-von-goethe">Goethe</a> in a famous audience. The city lived from trade, crafts and garden produce.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a> was besieged by Prussian troops and its French garrison surrendered in 1814. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> returned the city to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1815, as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-of-saxony">Province of Saxony</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-erfurt.jpg" alt="Principality of Erfurt" width="320" height="325"><figcaption>Image: Blank map of Europe.svg: maix First French Empire 1812.svg: Alphathon /'æɫfə.θɒn/ derivative work: OwenBlacker (talk) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:F%C3%BCrstentum_Erfurt_1812.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1806 CE – 1814 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Erfurt</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Napoleon I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism, Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Fürstentum Erfurt</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1810&amp;focus=principality-of-erfurt" aria-label="Principality of Erfurt on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4931 410 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Erfurt lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5251 666L5054 728M5251 666L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5251 666h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5251 666h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Erfurt. <a href="/atlas?year=1810&amp;focus=principality-of-erfurt">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
